\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Incidence adjoints in ordered indicator coordinates}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Take real finite incidence data with positive weights and the
[weighted adjoints](fdip-0007). Choose ordered vertex and edge
indicators and use the ordered-value maps
#{c_V:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^{|V|}} and
#{c_E:\mathbb R^E\to\mathbb R^{|E|}} from the
[incidence-matrix calculation](fdip-00AF). Write
#{B=c_EDc_V^{-1}}. The #{(v,e)} entry of
#{c_VD^*c_E^{-1}} is
##{\langle\delta_v,D^*\delta_e\rangle_V
=\langle D\delta_v,\delta_e\rangle_E=B_{ev},}
by the counting-adjoint identity. Thus #{c_VD^*c_E^{-1}=B^T}.
The endpoint formula in \ref{fdip-00JD} expresses
the same return map without ordering the indicators.
In these charts the multiplication map
#{W} has array #{W_c=c_EWc_E^{-1}=\operatorname{diag}(w(e))}, so
##{c_VD_w^\dagger c_E^{-1}=B^TW_c,\qquad
c_VLc_V^{-1}=-B^TW_cB.}
For a single edge #{0\to1} of weight #{c>0}, with vertex order
#{(0,1)}, the two adjoints send an edge value #{a} to
#{(-a,a)} and #{(-ca,ca)}, respectively.}

\p{Choose a subset of edges to reverse, and set
#{\varepsilon(e)=-1} on those edges and #{1} on the others.
The pointwise sign map #{(Ja)(e)=\varepsilon(e)a(e)} has array
#{S=c_EJc_E^{-1}=\operatorname{diag}(\varepsilon(e))}.
Reversing the endpoint difference gives #{B'=SB}, and
##{S^T=S,\qquad S^2=I,\qquad SW_cS=W_c,\qquad
(B')^TW_cB'=B^TW_cB.}
A loop has a zero incidence row before and after reversal.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.toMatrix_adjoint} identifies adjoint
matrices with conjugate transposes in orthonormal bases. Over the reals
this is transpose. The coordinate edge basis is orthonormal for counting,
but not generally for the weighted pairing; the return-map calculation
above accounts for the extra #{W_c}.}

\p{For unweighted simple graphs, Mathlib's
\leanref{SimpleGraph.posSemidef_lapMatrix} proves positivity of its
Laplacian. That model has no separate weighted edge set; its energy
formula sums both adjacency directions and divides by two. The
edge-once sum in \ref{fdip-0008} also handles loops and parallel
edges, and is proved there directly.}
